{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 7,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.137,
            0.046,
            0.346,
            0.063
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 8"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.087,
            0.829,
            0.124
          ],
          "content": "So, the length of the side of a square with an area of 49 sq. cm is 7 cm. We call 7 the **square root** of 49."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.125,
            0.622,
            0.145
          ],
          "content": "In general, $\\mathrm { i f }   y   \\stackrel { - } { = }   x ^ { 2 }$ then x is the **square root** of y."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.154,
            0.448,
            0.176
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "？ What is the square root of 64?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.179,
            0.848,
            0.215
          ],
          "content": "We know that $8 \\times 8$ is 64. So, 8 is the square root of 64. What about $- 8 \\times - 8 ?$ That is 64 too!"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.389,
            0.215,
            0.607,
            0.234
          ],
          "content": "8^{2}=64,\\mathrm{and}(-8)^{2}=64.",
          "image_source": "images/page_7_equation_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.234,
            0.549,
            0.253
          ],
          "content": "So, the square roots of 64 are + 8 and – 8."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.253,
            0.846,
            0.314
          ],
          "content": "Every perfect square has two integer square roots. One is positive and the other is negative. The square root of a number is denoted by Thus, ${ \\sqrt { 6 4 } }   =   \\pm 8$ and $\\sqrt { 1 0 0 }   =   \\pm 1 0$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.316,
            0.765,
            0.36
          ],
          "content": "Note that ${ \\sqrt { 8 ^ { 2 } } }   =   \\pm 8$ and $\\sqrt { 1 0 ^ { 2 } }   =   \\pm 1 0$ . In general, ${ \\sqrt { n ^ { 2 } } }   =   \\pm n$ In this chapter, we shall only consider the positive square root."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.368,
            0.801,
            0.426
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "？ Given a number, such as 576 or 327, how do we find out if it is a perfect square? If it is a perfect square, how can we find its square root?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.817,
            0.373,
            0.904,
            0.43
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_7_image_10.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.43,
            0.847,
            0.486
          ],
          "content": "We know that perfect squares end in 1, 4, 9, 6, 5, or an even number of zeros. But, it is not certain that a number that satisfies this condition is a square."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.485,
            0.847,
            0.524
          ],
          "content": "We can clearly say that 327 is not a perfect square. However, we cannot be sure that 576 is a perfect square."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.527,
            0.848,
            0.603
          ],
          "content": "1. We can list all the square numbers in sequence and find out whether 576 occurs among them. We know that 202 = 400, we can find squares of 21, 22, 23, … and so on until we get 576 or a number greater than 576."
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.202,
            0.611,
            0.294,
            0.631
          ],
          "content": "2 0 ^ { 2 } = 4 0 0",
          "image_source": "images/page_7_equation_14.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.328,
            0.612,
            0.417,
            0.631
          ],
          "content": "2 1 ^ { 2 } = 4 4 1",
          "image_source": "images/page_7_equation_15.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.453,
            0.611,
            0.546,
            0.631
          ],
          "content": "22^{2} = 484",
          "image_source": "images/page_7_equation_16.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.579,
            0.611,
            0.67,
            0.631
          ],
          "content": "2 3 ^ { 2 } = 5 2 9",
          "image_source": "images/page_7_equation_17.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.705,
            0.611,
            0.796,
            0.631
          ],
          "content": "2 4 ^ { 2 } = 5 7 6",
          "image_source": "images/page_7_equation_18.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.183,
            0.642,
            0.792,
            0.663
          ],
          "content": "However, this process becomes inefficient for larger numbers."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.672,
            0.847,
            0.711
          ],
          "content": "2. Recall that every square can be expressed as a sum of consecutive odd numbers starting from 1."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.186,
            0.716,
            0.325,
            0.736
          ],
          "content": "Consider $\\sqrt { 8 1 }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.189,
            0.757,
            0.296,
            0.775
          ],
          "content": "81 - 1 = 80",
          "image_source": "images/page_7_equation_22.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.311,
            0.757,
            0.416,
            0.775
          ],
          "content": "80 - 3 = 77",
          "image_source": "images/page_7_equation_23.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.559,
            0.757,
            0.664,
            0.775
          ],
          "content": "72 - 7 = 65",
          "image_source": "images/page_7_equation_24.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.69,
            0.757,
            0.794,
            0.775
          ],
          "content": "65 - 9 = 56",
          "image_source": "images/page_7_equation_25.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.189,
            0.786,
            0.306,
            0.806
          ],
          "content": "56 - 11 = 45",
          "image_source": "images/page_7_equation_26.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.332,
            0.787,
            0.447,
            0.806
          ],
          "content": "45 - 13 = 32",
          "image_source": "images/page_7_equation_27.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.478,
            0.786,
            0.594,
            0.806
          ],
          "content": "32 - 15 = 17",
          "image_source": "images/page_7_equation_28.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.628,
            0.787,
            0.733,
            0.806
          ],
          "content": "1 7 - 1 7 = 0",
          "image_source": "images/page_7_equation_29.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.185,
            0.827,
            0.847,
            0.87
          ],
          "content": "From 81, we successively subtracted consecutive odd numbers starting from 1 until we obtained 0 at the 9th step. Therefore ${ \\sqrt { 8 1 } }   = 9$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.185,
            0.873,
            0.848,
            0.912
          ],
          "content": "Can we find the square root of 729 using this method? Yes, but it will be time-consuming."
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.092,
            0.915,
            0.112,
            0.936
          ],
          "content": "8"
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
            0.449,
            0.97,
            0.551,
            0.985
          ],
          "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
        }
      ]
    }
